        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "7f446b18-2524-5f29-abb8-1ee69bcf8444",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Quonset Point, NAS Quonset Point, Davisville, North Kingstown, Rhode Island\n- Organizations: United States Navy, Naval Construction Battalions, Seabees\n- Facilities: Camp Endicott, Davisville Naval Construction Battalion Center\n- Products: Quonset hut\n- Events: World War II, National Register of Historic Places\n\nFacts:\n- Starting in World War II, the U.S. military used the Quonset hut as a standardized temporary structure.\n- Quonset Point gave its name to the Quonset hut.\n- Camp Endicott was a United States Navy Seabee facility that was part of the Davisville Naval Construction Battalion Center at Quonset Point in North Kingstown, Rhode Island.\n- Camp Endicott at Davisville was next to NAS Quonset Point and was home to the Naval Construction Battalions known as the Seabees.\n- In 1978, a surviving portion of Camp Endicott, which was mostly demolished, was listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "5ecfec00-b238-57ec-8513-45fa4b8556c5",
        "text": "Page: Quonset Point Air National Guard Station. Sentence: Next to NAS Quonset Point was Camp Endicott at Davisville, home of the Naval Construction Battalions known as the Seabees. Quonset Point also gave its name to the Quonset hut, a standardized temporary structure used by the U.S. military starting in World War II.\nPage: Camp Endicott. Sentence: Camp Endicott, was a United States Navy Seabee facility, part of Davisville Naval Construction Battalion Center at Quonset Point in North Kingstown, Rhode Island.\nPage: Camp Endicott. Sentence: A surviving portion of the camp, now mostly demolished, was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1978.",
